speechtext:
-other members who have preceded me in this debate have supported the bill in principle. The explanatory note reads as follows:
The purpose of this bill is to provide that all employees in Canada who come under federal labour jurisdiction shall receive their regular pay for at least eight statutory holidays each year, without having to work on those holidays. It also provides that when any such employee is required to work on any statutory holiday, as defined in the bill, he shall receive pay for such work at double time in addition to his regular pay for such holiday.
Nothing in this bill affects any provision for statutory holidays with pay enjoyed by any employees where such provisions are more favourable than those enjoyed herein, but this bill does supersede any provisions which are less favourable than those set out in this bill.
I might say, Mr. Speaker, that I think the principle of the bill is a laudable one. However, I am inclined to agree with the hon. member for Vancouver South (Mr. Broome) who said that payment for statutory holidays was a matter of negotiation and should

be left to bargaining between labour and management. It seems to me that if you attempt to force industry to make payment for holidays you are not really serving the purpose for which the bill was actually designed. The bill sets out that extra remuneration shall be paid to those who work on holidays but I think that implicit in that is the intention to discourage work on such holidays. The hon. member referred to clause 3 of the bill which has to do with the application of the measure. That clause rdads as follows:
This act applies to and in respect of employment upon or in connection with any work, undertaking or business that is within the legislative authority of the parliament of Canada, including, but not so as to restrict the generality of the foregoing,
(a)	works, undertakings, or businesses operated or carried on for or in connection with navigation and shipping, whether inland or maritime, including the operation of ships and transportation by ship anywhere in Canada;
(b)	railways, canals, telegraphs and other works and undertakings connecting a province with any other or others of the provinces, or extending beyond the limits of a province;
(c)	lines of steam and other ships connecting a province with any other or others of the provinces or extending beyond the limits of a province;
(d)	ferries between any province and any other province-
